        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The circadian timing system (CTS) has been shown to be involved in the coordinated daily variation of almost every physiological and psychological system evaluated thus far. Maintaining synchronized circadian rhythms is important to health and well-being. A growing body of research indicates that desynchronization of the circadian system can be detrimental to mental and physical health. Given the increasing duration of space missions as well as their concomitant increased confinement and isolation of crewmembers, problems associated with changes of the circadian rhythm might be increasingly prominent during future exploratory missions. The aim of the study is to assess the impact of long-duration isolation on temperature regulation and circadian rhythm using NASA’s Human Exploration Research Analog (HERA) facility. Investigators hypothesize that circadian rhythm will be disrupted by long-duration isolation. Specifically, it is suggested that due to reduced physical activity and/or changes in autonomic control of the autonomic nervous system, and the absence of 24-hour light and dark cycles, circadian rhythms will be structurally altered and characterized by strong inter-individual differences. It is assumed that it will be possible to document and quantify these changes by simultaneous recordings of 36-hours of core body temperature (CBT) determined by both a non-invasive heatflux sensor (Double Sensor) as well as gut temperature using an ingestible temperature pill. In addition, CBT will be corrected for physical activity, ambient temperature and light exposure, sleep-wake periods and posture. Finally, in light of the facts that the origin of the circadian rhythm of CBT is predominantly controlled by circadian changes in the rate of loss of heat through the extremities, and peripheral cutaneous tone can be expected to be altered during space flight, superficial temperatures could promote the understanding of any alterations of the circadian rhythm of CBT during isolation.
<p>
Specific Aims:<ol><br>
<li>Determination of endogenous circadian rhythm in isolation and confinement over 30 days.
<li>Determination of modificatory elements to the circadian rhythm like heart rate, light level, light spectra , changes in daily routine, and physical and psychological strain.
<li>Comparability of ground-based data and those from space.</ol>
<p>
The results of this study can be used to detect possible changes in the circadian rhythm during isolation and their onset time, and allow the development of a new, non-invasive method for detecting the core body temperature in the clinical and non-clinical environment. In the space sector, the results of study can serve to detect negative impairments during future exploratory space missions as well as developing effective countermeasures.
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
In this study, a total of 32 healthy adults with be randomly assigned to one of the following two groups:<p>
1)  Intervention group (spending 30 days in the HERA habitat)<br>
2)  Control group (unchanged working and living conditions)<br>
During four research missions, a total of 96 measurements will be performed on each of the subjects. The measurements at the beginning of the study record general physiological data: height, body weight, body mass index (BMI), waist to hip ratio, and blood pressure.
<p>
During the study the following measurements will be carried out: core body temperature (via radio pill and double sensor), surface temperature (via skin sensors), activity and sleep behavior via continuous collection with the Actiwatch and Actigraph systems. The evaluation of recorded data will aim to provide information on if, when, and how changes of activity and sleep patterns occur relative to changes in the circadian rhythm and core temperature in isolation and confinement.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
This experiment is in progress. Results will be available at a later date.
<p>
